 to=self<|message|>Write a short story about that teaches the reader about the importance of love. Make sure story includes these words: pillow, cloud, exaggerating

Write short story. Include words pillow, cloud, exaggerating. Teach importance of love.

Let's produce story.<|start|>assistant to=user<|message|>Mara had been exaggerating for years.

Not about big things. About small ones. That the coffee was cold. That the bus was always late. That her neighbor's dog barked too much. She told herself it was just honesty, but really it was armor. If she complained first, no one could hurt her.

When her mother got sick, Mara moved back into the small house they grew up in. The bedroom was still hers, with the same faded quilt and the same lumpy pillow she had slept on since college. She sat on the edge of the bed at night while her mother slept in the recliner downstairs, hooked to an oxygen machine that hissed like a tired cat.

One night the power went out. The whole block went dark. Mara found a flashlight and went downstairs. Her mother was awake, panicked, trying to find the tank.

"It's okay, it's okay," Mara said, her voice thin. "The power will be back. It's probably just a few minutes."

Her mother grabbed her wrist. "Don't lie to me."

Mara froze. She had spent a lifetime exaggerating the bad so she wouldn't be surprised by it. Now she realized she had been doing the opposite with love — minimizing it, making it small and practical, so it wouldn't matter when it was gone.

"I don't know when it'll be back," Mara said quietly. "I'm scared too."

Her mother squeezed her hand. "Then sit with me."

They sat on the floor in the dark, backs against the couch, the flashlight between them. Mara rested her head on her mother's shoulder. The pillow she had brought down was too small for both of them, so they shared it, cheek to cheek.

Outside, a cloud passed over the moon and the room went completely black for a second. In that blackness, Mara heard her mother's breathing, uneven but steady, and her own.

"I love you," her mother whispered.

Mara had heard it a thousand times as a child, but she had stopped listening as an adult, assuming it was background noise. Now it landed like something solid.

"I love you too," she said. "I'm sorry I made everything sound worse than it was."

Her mother smiled. "You were just trying to protect yourself."

The power came back on at 2 a.m. The machine hummed. The refrigerator kicked. The world was lit again.

Mara didn't move. She stayed there on the floor, holding her mother's hand, learning that love wasn't a grand speech or a perfect moment. It was choosing to sit in the dark without exaggerating the fear, and still saying the words out loud. [end of text]


